[3/3] Concurrency demos (race bug, exclusion fix, FOR UPDATE, SERIALIZABLE, isolation, deadlock) - two real sessions...

=== PART 1 - THE BUG: check-then-insert with no protection (both sessions want pitch 1, slot 10) ===
  team A: slot looks free = True
  team B: slot looks free = True
  results: {'B': 'inserted', 'A': 'inserted'}
  rows for the same slot: 2  -> DOUBLE BOOKING (bug reproduced)

=== PART 2 - FIX 1: booking_no_overlap EXCLUDE constraint on the real booking table ===
  session A: slot looks free = True
  session B: slot looks free = True
  results: {'B': 'booking inserted', 'A': 'ExclusionViolation: conflicting key value violates exclusion constraint "booking_no_overlap"'}
  confirmed bookings for that slot: 1 (must be 1)

=== PART 3 - FIX 2: SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on a lock row, then check, then insert ===
  team A: got the lock
  team B: got the lock
  results: {'A': 'inserted', 'B': "refused (slot already taken - the second session saw the first one's row)"}
  rows for the slot: 1 (must be 1)

=== PART 4 - FIX 3: SERIALIZABLE isolation (one session is aborted with 'could not serialize') ===
  results: {'B': 'SerializationFailure: could not serialize access due to read/write dependencies among transactions', 'A': 'committed'}
  rows for the slot: 1 (must be 1)

=== PART 5 - READ COMMITTED vs REPEATABLE READ (price read twice while another session updates it) ===
  READ COMMITTED  : first read 9000, other session sets 12000 and commits, second read 12000  -> non-repeatable read
  REPEATABLE READ : first read 9000, other session sets 12000 and commits, second read 9000  -> repeatable

=== PART 6 - DEADLOCK: two transfers lock accounts in opposite order ===
  results: {'B': 'ABORTED by PostgreSQL: deadlock detected', 'A': 'committed'}
  fix = always lock in the same order (lowest id first); balances now: [(1, 99), (2, 101)]

Done. All lines above are real output from this run.

